---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'What is a Stablecoin Depeg? — Stable or Gone Guide';
const description = 'A plain-language guide to stablecoin depeg events, recovered depegs, major depegs, collapse outcomes, and how SOG separates events from failed status.';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'What is a Stablecoin Depeg?',
  description,
  url: 'https://sog.badjoke-lab.com/guides/what-is-a-depeg/',
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Stable or Gone', url: 'https://sog.badjoke-lab.com/' },
  about: ['stablecoin depeg', 'stablecoin collapse', 'stablecoin event history']
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/guides/what-is-a-depeg/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="panel hero-main">
      <div class="prompt">&gt; guide --depeg</div>
      <h1>What is a Depeg?</h1>
      <p>A depeg is a material deviation from a stablecoin's target peg. In SOG, a depeg is an event, not a status label by itself.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">plain-language definition</div>
    <p>
      A stablecoin depeg happens when a token designed to track a reference asset, usually USD, trades or redeems away from that target in a way that is historically relevant. SOG does not treat every tiny price movement as a recordable event. The registry focuses on events with source-backed context: issuer statements, exchange notices, reserve disclosures, regulatory records, research sources, or widely documented market stress.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key distinction is outcome. A stablecoin can experience a depeg and later recover, as recorded for <a href="/stablecoin/usdc/">USDC</a>. Another stablecoin can lose peg and fail as a functioning stablecoin, as recorded for <a href="/stablecoin/ust/">UST / TerraUSD</a>. Those outcomes should not be collapsed into the same label.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">SOG handling</div>
    <table><tbody>
      <tr><th>Minor movement</th><td>Small or short-lived market movements are usually not recorded as standalone events.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Notable depeg</th><td>A depeg can be recorded when it is material, sustained, historically relevant, or supported by public sources.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Major depeg</th><td>A major depeg should include context such as trigger, recovery, source list, duration if supportable, and known unknowns.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Collapse</th><td>A collapse is a severe lifecycle outcome. It requires stronger source support than a normal market deviation.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">important distinction</div>
    <table><tbody>
      <tr><th>Recovered depeg</th><td>A recovered depeg does not automatically make the stablecoin failed. USDC's March 2023 event is treated this way.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Failed status</th><td>Failed status is used only when the lifecycle and evidence support that outcome, such as UST after the May 2022 collapse.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>No live price feed</th><td>SOG does not try to be a live peg monitor. Historical price values should be source-specific when used.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </section>

  <section class="section-grid" aria-label="Related records">
    <a class="panel mini-card" href="/event/sog_ev_usdc_2023_03_depeg/"><h2>USDC March 2023 depeg</h2><p>Recovered depeg event with banking exposure context.</p></a>
    <a class="panel mini-card" href="/event/sog_ev_ust_2022_05_collapse/"><h2>UST collapse</h2><p>Failed lifecycle outcome, separated from post-collapse USTC trading.</p></a>
    <a class="panel mini-card" href="/guides/status-vs-event/"><h2>Status vs Event</h2><p>Why depeg is an event and failed is a lifecycle status.</p></a>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">related</div>
    <table><tbody>
      <tr><th>Read next</th><td><a href="/guides/status-vs-event/">Stablecoin Status vs Event</a> · <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> · <a href="/events/">Events</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
